1
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:10,133
[engine starts]

2
00:00:10,844 --> 00:00:14,080
-[Jerry] This is a 1969 Pontiac GTO.
-[engine revving]

3
00:00:16,016 --> 00:00:17,584
But not just any GTO.

4
00:00:17,666 --> 00:00:20,266
This... is the Judge.

5
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:21,721
[rock music playing]

6
00:00:27,627 --> 00:00:31,131
The name "GTO" was stolen
from the Ferrari GTO of the early '60s.

7
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:36,600
But in America, in 1969, we thought,
"Ah, they're all the way over in Italy.

8
00:00:36,636 --> 00:00:38,705
-They'll never catch us."
-[tires screech]

9
00:00:38,772 --> 00:00:41,307
And "the Judge" was
from Sammy Davis Jr.'s character

10
00:00:41,374 --> 00:00:42,909
on Rowan &amp; Martin's Laugh-In.

11
00:00:43,109 --> 00:00:45,378
Here comes the judge!
Here comes the judge!

12
00:00:45,445 --> 00:00:47,614
[Jerry] I guess in 1969,
Pontiac's attitude was,

13
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:50,383
"Copyright?
Screw it, we'll decide what's right."

14
00:00:50,450 --> 00:00:52,118
[engine starts and revs]

15
00:00:52,266 --> 00:00:55,199
Especially with a blacked-out grille,
hideaway headlights,

16
00:00:55,266 --> 00:00:58,099
a tach on the hood, a rear spoiler,

17
00:00:58,166 --> 00:01:02,500
and open-hood scoops into a four-barrel,
370-horsepower V-8.

18
00:01:02,562 --> 00:01:03,696
[engine revving]

19
00:01:05,031 --> 00:01:07,200
-[Howard] Hello?
-[Jerry] Howard?

20
00:01:07,467 --> 00:01:09,936
[Howard] Jerry Seinfeld. Oh, my God.

21
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:13,566
[Jerry] Remember when you were trying
to make it and I came on your show?

22
00:01:13,633 --> 00:01:16,666
-[Howard] Right, right, and I--
-[Jerry] Or was it the other way around?

23
00:01:16,733 --> 00:01:19,333
[Howard] I don't know. It doesn't matter.
I gotta be honest.

24
00:01:19,379 --> 00:01:21,214
I don't wanna go
to a coffee shop with you.

25
00:01:21,281 --> 00:01:23,783
[Jerry] I don't give a damn. You're stuck.

26
00:01:23,850 --> 00:01:26,019
-[Howard] You have nothing prepared?
-[Jerry] Nothing.

27
00:01:26,719 --> 00:01:28,555
[Howard] I can't believe we're doing this.

28
00:01:32,633 --> 00:01:33,933
[Jerry] I'm Jerry Seinfeld,

29
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,066
and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

30
00:01:38,833 --> 00:01:41,500
A GTO is for a guy that wants
to tell the world,

31
00:01:41,568 --> 00:01:43,002
"I will not be going quietly."

32
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:48,199
Which is why it's a perfect car
for my guest this week, Mr. Howard Stern,

33
00:01:48,266 --> 00:01:49,300
who, according to him,

34
00:01:49,342 --> 00:01:51,611
is the greatest radio broadcaster
of all time.

35
00:01:51,978 --> 00:01:52,879
[tires screech]

36
00:01:52,946 --> 00:01:54,814
Let's get the coffee
and get this over with.

37
00:01:55,615 --> 00:01:57,550
Look at the people rejoicing
that we're here.

38
00:01:57,617 --> 00:01:59,085
This is fun. Let's go.

39
00:02:03,156 --> 00:02:04,824
[Howard] Here's where it started for me.

40
00:02:04,891 --> 00:02:07,227
This radio was everything to my father.

41
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:09,000
He would ignore me the entire time.

42
00:02:09,066 --> 00:02:11,500
He would tell me to shush the whole time
and ignore me.

43
00:02:11,564 --> 00:02:13,533
-And I became enamored with radio.
-Wow.

44
00:02:14,334 --> 00:02:16,002
I could get my father's attention,

45
00:02:16,069 --> 00:02:18,204
if I could get in that box
and get on the radio.

46
00:02:18,571 --> 00:02:21,174
And so this was my way
of getting my dad's attention.

47
00:02:21,908 --> 00:02:23,810
Hey, I'm opening up. Hi, ladies.

48
00:02:25,745 --> 00:02:28,882
I feel this is the most unimportant show
in show business that I'm on right now.

49
00:02:28,948 --> 00:02:30,416
Then that's another win for me.

50
00:02:30,483 --> 00:02:33,453
I got you on the most unimportant show
in show business.

51
00:02:33,753 --> 00:02:36,022
Well,
I'm honored that you asked me, actually.

52
00:02:36,289 --> 00:02:38,591
-Really?
-Yeah. I really am, because I admire you.

53
00:02:38,892 --> 00:02:41,261
-[softly] Thank you.
-And I love what you do.

54
00:02:41,327 --> 00:02:44,864
I'm a fan of musicians and comics
and all these people,

55
00:02:44,931 --> 00:02:48,034
and I love doing an interview
with people like yourself

56
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:49,866
-or Graham Nash or Paul McCartney.
-Mm-hmm.

57
00:02:52,238 --> 00:02:55,942
I would say that therapy helped me
to appreciate what I have.

58
00:02:56,009 --> 00:02:57,844
[Jerry] Do you think therapy
could help me?

59
00:02:57,911 --> 00:02:58,945
[Howard] I absolutely do.

60
00:02:59,012 --> 00:03:01,481
-What could I possibly be better at?
-A lot of things.

61
00:03:01,548 --> 00:03:02,849
Do I seem to have problems?

62
00:03:04,651 --> 00:03:07,186
Can you pee in front of other men?
Do you have trouble?

63
00:03:07,766 --> 00:03:09,933
-No.
-I have a huge issue with that.

64
00:03:09,989 --> 00:03:12,458
I can't. If you and I had to go pee,
it would be terrible.

65
00:03:12,525 --> 00:03:15,094
-Let's go pee together today.
-No. I'm not doing it.

66
00:03:15,361 --> 00:03:17,297
-Well, you've talked to me privately.
-Yeah.

67
00:03:17,363 --> 00:03:19,766
It's awkward talking to me, right?
It's uncomfortable.

68
00:03:19,832 --> 00:03:21,668
-No, I like talking... Yeah.
-Really?

69
00:03:21,734 --> 00:03:23,636
I think people just want to get away.

70
00:03:23,703 --> 00:03:25,939
-From you?
-Yeah. Unless we're on the radio.

71
00:03:26,005 --> 00:03:29,609
Maybe if you didn't have the little hairs
in front of the lens of the glasses.

72
00:03:29,700 --> 00:03:32,299
-What am I gonna do about it?
-You might seem more open.

73
00:03:32,345 --> 00:03:33,179
Maybe I'm hiding.

74
00:03:33,413 --> 00:03:36,015
You're like a hip
Wicked Witch of the West.

75
00:03:38,333 --> 00:03:40,233
Look at this Yenta here, Howard,
on the phone.

76
00:03:40,333 --> 00:03:41,866
Look at the bent hand...

77
00:03:41,921 --> 00:03:43,656
Is there any difference
between a baby with a bottle

78
00:03:43,723 --> 00:03:45,358
and a woman walking, a grown woman

79
00:03:45,433 --> 00:03:49,033
who can't [beeps] walk five steps
without her cell phone on?

80
00:03:49,266 --> 00:03:51,333
Everybody's proud of themselves.

81
00:03:51,399 --> 00:03:54,833
Everybody thinks their life
is the big thing that's going on.

82
00:03:54,933 --> 00:03:57,533
Right. Even though we know my life
is the big thing going on.

83
00:03:57,570 --> 00:03:58,938
-Why would they think that?
-[laughs]

84
00:04:01,107 --> 00:04:04,611
What do you want to do, do you think,
the last year of your life?

85
00:04:05,178 --> 00:04:07,347
I don't know.
I'm obsessed with that, are you?

86
00:04:07,647 --> 00:04:09,582
-No.
-Just give me some time.

87
00:04:11,317 --> 00:04:13,953
I'm in love with my wife,
I do want to spend time with her.

88
00:04:14,020 --> 00:04:15,788
You're like a woman to me
in a sense that...

89
00:04:15,855 --> 00:04:18,024
-I don't have to be...
-That sounds like a Graham Nash song.

90
00:04:18,424 --> 00:04:21,494
No, because you're a comedian,
every minute you're evaluating.

91
00:04:21,561 --> 00:04:23,263
-You're judgmental.
-That's right.

92
00:04:24,733 --> 00:04:27,266
The comedian studies himself.

93
00:04:27,333 --> 00:04:29,800
-Yes.
-The actor studies other people.

94
00:04:29,866 --> 00:04:31,800
-That's true.
-The comedian

95
00:04:31,866 --> 00:04:33,033
wants to be himself.

96
00:04:33,072 --> 00:04:34,574
The actor wants to be anyone

97
00:04:34,641 --> 00:04:36,542
-but themselves.
-You're so true.

98
00:04:36,609 --> 00:04:37,977
Because I feel tortured.

99
00:04:38,166 --> 00:04:40,566
I'm so self-conscious, so self-aware.

100
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:43,000
-I would like...
-You're not tortured.

101
00:04:43,049 --> 00:04:44,717
...to see you make some progress.

102
00:04:44,784 --> 00:04:47,453
-But you don't experience that.
-Not so self-conscious.

103
00:04:48,087 --> 00:04:50,590
-Okay. It's not about your looks, Howard.
-But you are...

104
00:04:50,657 --> 00:04:52,091
Don't you get that by now?

105
00:04:52,166 --> 00:04:53,533
-Nobody cares...
-Yeah, but...

106
00:04:53,593 --> 00:04:54,694
...what your hair looks like.

107
00:04:54,761 --> 00:04:56,963
That's exactly why I go to therapy.

108
00:04:57,033 --> 00:04:58,800
-I understand that intellectually.
-I'm telling you,

109
00:04:58,865 --> 00:05:00,667
you can go to me,
you don't need the therapy.

110
00:05:00,733 --> 00:05:02,035
Let me ask you something.

111
00:05:02,099 --> 00:05:03,300
You didn't answer my question.

112
00:05:03,366 --> 00:05:05,566
-Wait a second.
-You're not a good talk-show guest.

113
00:05:05,605 --> 00:05:07,507
When you did your sitcom, okay?

114
00:05:07,807 --> 00:05:09,442
If America hadn't tuned in,

115
00:05:09,509 --> 00:05:11,411
wouldn't you have cared
what America thought?

116
00:05:11,477 --> 00:05:13,913
Of course. Yes. That's different
than caring about my hair.

117
00:05:14,747 --> 00:05:17,016
-It's the same exact thing.
-It's the complete opposite!

118
00:05:17,083 --> 00:05:21,220
You're saying that we want to get
to a point as people, as adults...

119
00:05:21,287 --> 00:05:23,656
As long as we feel good
about how we feel...

120
00:05:23,723 --> 00:05:25,024
No, no, no. You misunderstood me.

121
00:05:26,492 --> 00:05:29,062
So, on the radio, should I worry
about what other people think?

122
00:05:29,128 --> 00:05:31,197
-Yes. You should...
-Should I worry about what I think?

123
00:05:31,564 --> 00:05:33,700
You should worry
that they're enjoying the show or not.

124
00:05:34,033 --> 00:05:36,803
-Same as worrying about your hair.
-But if people like my stupid hair

125
00:05:36,869 --> 00:05:39,706
-or how fat I am.
-It's the same [beeps] thing.

126
00:05:41,533 --> 00:05:43,566
Your hair is not an achievement.

127
00:05:43,633 --> 00:05:46,099
Yours perhaps is,
but most people's hair is not.

128
00:05:46,145 --> 00:05:47,347
My hair is an achievement.

129
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:49,482
Did you have a hair person
before this show?

130
00:05:49,549 --> 00:05:51,984
-Absolutely, I did. No, I'm--
-You're a radio guy.

131
00:05:53,753 --> 00:05:55,922
I mean, honestly, I want to know
how you are sexually.

132
00:05:55,988 --> 00:05:58,558
-Why? This is another gay thing.
-Well, because it's interesting.

133
00:05:58,624 --> 00:06:00,793
No, stop it.
Why is everything gay with you?

134
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:04,731
I like "gay."
I'm not comfortable talking about this.

135
00:06:04,797 --> 00:06:06,099
That's why this is great.

136
00:06:06,166 --> 00:06:07,166
[laughs]

137
00:06:07,533 --> 00:06:09,669
This is why your show
is the top show on the Internet.

138
00:06:09,736 --> 00:06:10,570
[Jerry claps]

139
00:06:11,604 --> 00:06:12,638
[Howard] Let's go.

140
00:06:12,705 --> 00:06:14,807
It's enough already.
I'm worn out from you.

141
00:06:15,274 --> 00:06:16,442
That's an accomplishment.

142
00:06:17,410 --> 00:06:20,012
Hey, now! See? People are nice.

143
00:06:23,916 --> 00:06:25,952
[Jerry] If you could have anything here,
what would you have?

144
00:06:26,018 --> 00:06:27,520
-I like this. The chocolate...
-[Jerry] Really?

145
00:06:27,587 --> 00:06:29,522
-[Jerry] Chocolate cake.
-[Howard] Chocolate anything.

146
00:06:29,589 --> 00:06:31,357
Chocolate with peanut butter. Oh.

147
00:06:31,424 --> 00:06:32,859
-Oh, let's do it.
-Let's do it.

148
00:06:34,026 --> 00:06:36,596
-We're gonna get him a brownie.
-No.

149
00:06:36,662 --> 00:06:38,431
I don't want a brownie.
I'm not gonna eat it.

150
00:06:38,564 --> 00:06:40,466
-Don't waste your time.
-[waiter] Coffee?

151
00:06:40,533 --> 00:06:42,135
-Yes.
-[Howard] He's gonna have coffee.

152
00:06:42,201 --> 00:06:45,505
-I'm gonna have a cup of hot water.
-[waiter] Fine. I'll be right back.

153
00:06:45,599 --> 00:06:48,533
Oh, God! This is like being with my aunt
from the old country.

154
00:06:50,009 --> 00:06:52,812
How long can you actually,
physically stay focused on the kids?

155
00:06:53,200 --> 00:06:57,200
I can't play Monopoly.
I cannot play Chutes and Ladders.

156
00:06:57,250 --> 00:06:58,918
I'd rather someone shoot me on a ladder.

157
00:06:58,985 --> 00:07:01,988
Sorry!, to me,
is the most perfectly named game.

158
00:07:02,255 --> 00:07:03,923
I've never felt so sorry.

159
00:07:05,158 --> 00:07:07,393
Your career is a victory over the self.

160
00:07:07,460 --> 00:07:09,996
It wasn't the business.
It wasn't other people.

161
00:07:10,263 --> 00:07:12,431
It wasn't your relationships,
your wife, whatever.

162
00:07:12,498 --> 00:07:14,233
None of that was the biggest obstacle.

163
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:16,269
It was you. You conquered yourself.

164
00:07:16,700 --> 00:07:20,900
But when I evaluate myself as a radio guy,
I think I'm the best that ever lived.

165
00:07:20,940 --> 00:07:22,575
-[laughs]
-Ever.

166
00:07:22,642 --> 00:07:24,477
Are you better than Jay Leno
as a stand-up?

167
00:07:24,777 --> 00:07:25,912
That's a stupid question.

168
00:07:28,247 --> 00:07:31,083
Do you think you're one of the best
stand-ups that ever lived?

169
00:07:31,317 --> 00:07:34,754
-Would you put yourself in the top five?
-Comedy is more personal than food.

170
00:07:37,957 --> 00:07:39,992
Someone either hits you funny
or they don't.

171
00:07:40,059 --> 00:07:41,260
I never gave a [beeps]

172
00:07:41,327 --> 00:07:43,930
I would go on and say
whatever the hell was--

173
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,166
Well, you're alone in the room.

174
00:07:47,099 --> 00:07:48,968
-True. It's different.
-You just noticed?

175
00:07:50,403 --> 00:07:52,071
When he goes on talk shows now...

176
00:07:52,138 --> 00:07:56,275
Okay, all right, let's not get
into character assassination, okay?

177
00:07:56,366 --> 00:07:57,966
-Who are you, Jesus?
-[laughs]

178
00:07:58,299 --> 00:08:00,900
Calm down. I want to get negative.
Let me go there.

179
00:08:00,966 --> 00:08:01,966
What kind of interviewer are you?

180
00:08:02,033 --> 00:08:04,299
You have to let me get negative.
You have to let me be me.

181
00:08:04,350 --> 00:08:06,519
-You be you and I'll be me.
-Great idea.

182
00:08:06,586 --> 00:08:08,421
-You're so judgmental. I told you.
-I am.

183
00:08:09,889 --> 00:08:11,491
-[Howard] Donald Trump.
-Do you like him?

184
00:08:11,566 --> 00:08:14,299
I think he's one
of the best radio guests ever.

185
00:08:14,427 --> 00:08:17,063
He comes on, and he will say whatever

186
00:08:17,163 --> 00:08:18,164
-is on his mind.
-Yeah.

187
00:08:18,231 --> 00:08:21,334
Some of the best interviews with him,
that you would never do this, but...

188
00:08:21,567 --> 00:08:22,635
He sits down and I go,

189
00:08:22,702 --> 00:08:24,670
"Donald,
I don't care about financial advice,

190
00:08:24,737 --> 00:08:25,838
I don't care about that."

191
00:08:25,905 --> 00:08:27,473
-Gee, I wonder why.
-I say,

192
00:08:27,566 --> 00:08:30,200
"Talk to me about women." [clears throat]

193
00:08:30,266 --> 00:08:31,299
And I will name women

194
00:08:31,366 --> 00:08:34,166
and he will evaluate them
on a scale of one to ten.

195
00:08:34,233 --> 00:08:36,066
-And he is brutal.
-He's brutal.

196
00:08:38,918 --> 00:08:41,320
Who don't you like, then?
Let's see you get a little negative.

197
00:08:41,387 --> 00:08:43,456
I don't wanna hear myself
talk like that.

198
00:08:43,523 --> 00:08:46,359
I'm not interested in what you like.
I wanna hear you say it.

199
00:08:46,433 --> 00:08:49,033
-That's good.
-Who cares? I can't believe you.

200
00:08:49,100 --> 00:08:52,100
This is what makes the great Howard Stern.
I'm having a good time.

201
00:08:52,166 --> 00:08:53,800
-You're having fun.
-Yeah. Yeah.

202
00:08:53,866 --> 00:08:55,668
Yeah. I think I'm your best interview.

203
00:08:55,735 --> 00:08:58,371
Would you like to play God with shows
and make shows, and say,

204
00:08:58,437 --> 00:09:01,240
"I like this guy, I want to do this"?
How come you haven't done that?

205
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:03,900
I had a production company
and I had ten shows.

206
00:09:03,966 --> 00:09:06,266
Sold and in production.
And I walked away from it.

207
00:09:06,333 --> 00:09:08,333
There's nothing more exciting
than the announcement.

208
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:10,633
"Howard Stern now
is the head of his own network."

209
00:09:10,699 --> 00:09:12,633
-Or whatever. Some big announcement.
-Yeah.

210
00:09:12,699 --> 00:09:13,966
That's a great high.

211
00:09:14,033 --> 00:09:16,000
But then you have to go out
and get it done.

212
00:09:16,066 --> 00:09:17,633
-Right.
-And then you have no life.

213
00:09:17,690 --> 00:09:19,959
And that's the problem.
I love the announcement.

214
00:09:20,026 --> 00:09:21,727
-It's so funny how it works.
-Yeah.

215
00:09:21,794 --> 00:09:24,163
You get that thing,
and you don't want it.

216
00:09:24,363 --> 00:09:27,533
-No.
-Why is life so [beeps] twisted like that?

217
00:09:27,600 --> 00:09:29,335
I don't know.
I figured you had the answer.

218
00:09:30,736 --> 00:09:33,105
Do you have trouble relating to people?

219
00:09:33,606 --> 00:09:36,242
Do you feel you're fully involved,
when you're around people?

220
00:09:37,543 --> 00:09:38,811
I'm sorry, I wasn't...

221
00:09:38,878 --> 00:09:40,813
See, I'm sitting here thinking

222
00:09:40,880 --> 00:09:44,383
you're really not enjoying this,
and you're disappointed in this.

223
00:09:44,450 --> 00:09:46,419
-But that's you.
-That's why I see a shrink.

224
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:48,166
But when I tell you that you're wrong,

225
00:09:48,220 --> 00:09:50,656
-you believe me, right?
-No. No.

226
00:09:50,723 --> 00:09:52,224
Because I'm too nice a guy?

227
00:09:52,291 --> 00:09:53,659
I don't think of you as a nice guy.

228
00:09:53,733 --> 00:09:56,933
-Thank you for that. [laughs]
-I think you are very brutally honest,

229
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,033
and I think that you can be brutal.

230
00:09:59,098 --> 00:10:01,133
I'm the nice guy sitting at this table

231
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:03,235
and you are absolutely mean.

232
00:10:03,402 --> 00:10:04,470
You just made my day.

233
00:10:04,537 --> 00:10:07,239
-Sell me the idea of therapy.
-You would sit down...

234
00:10:07,306 --> 00:10:11,477
For me the catalyst was my relationship
with my wife wasn't going well,

235
00:10:11,544 --> 00:10:14,513
things were falling apart,
and I needed guidance.

236
00:10:14,580 --> 00:10:16,382
That's great for you, but what about me?

237
00:10:16,449 --> 00:10:17,483
There's something going on.

238
00:10:17,550 --> 00:10:19,518
It's written all over your face.
Everyone sees it.

239
00:10:21,921 --> 00:10:25,224
I would've loved if my father had sat me
down and just said, "How are you?

240
00:10:25,291 --> 00:10:26,959
-How are you feeling?"
-Right, right.

241
00:10:27,026 --> 00:10:28,961
It seems like such a small thing.

242
00:10:29,028 --> 00:10:31,197
-But it's huge.
-How are you doing?

243
00:10:32,466 --> 00:10:34,166
That does touch me.

244
00:10:34,233 --> 00:10:37,003
Because that's something
that I would enjoy.

245
00:10:37,403 --> 00:10:38,637
I think.

246
00:10:38,704 --> 00:10:41,607
Although I'm so well-defended
against that.

247
00:10:41,674 --> 00:10:42,908
I see through your defenses.

248
00:10:42,975 --> 00:10:44,844
Did you have a good relationship
with your father?

249
00:10:44,910 --> 00:10:47,246
Yeah, I mean, I didn't expect much.

250
00:10:47,313 --> 00:10:49,215
Right. By the way, I love my father.

251
00:10:49,281 --> 00:10:52,184
A guy of his generation
didn't know how to go there and say...

252
00:10:52,251 --> 00:10:54,053
-He was so busy working.
-Yes.

253
00:10:54,120 --> 00:10:55,888
But as a kid, it would've been nice...

254
00:10:55,955 --> 00:10:58,691
I don't usually say this,
but you're right, it would've been nice.

255
00:10:58,758 --> 00:11:00,660
That's the gift I want to give
to my children.

256
00:11:00,726 --> 00:11:04,597
"Go out. I don't care
if you're working at a Wendy's.

257
00:11:05,097 --> 00:11:07,366
Be greatest at it.
Just take such pride in it."

258
00:11:07,433 --> 00:11:08,834
There's no "greatest" at Wendy's.

259
00:11:09,935 --> 00:11:12,238
Tell me I have a shot that
we'll have a brownie.

260
00:11:12,304 --> 00:11:14,373
-Get the brownie.
-I'm not gonna do it by myself.

261
00:11:14,440 --> 00:11:16,709
-All right, I'll have some.
-We're gonna share a brownie.

262
00:11:16,776 --> 00:11:18,611
-[waiter] Want me to cut it?
-We'll do it.

263
00:11:18,678 --> 00:11:21,280
I think it would be a little too romantic
if we do it.

264
00:11:21,347 --> 00:11:23,049
[Howard] No, we're not cutters...

265
00:11:23,115 --> 00:11:25,017
-We were...
-No, we weren't...

266
00:11:25,084 --> 00:11:26,352
I don't want to be romantic.

267
00:11:26,419 --> 00:11:27,853
[Jerry] You just want to know
how I am in bed.

268
00:11:27,920 --> 00:11:29,522
-I'm excited.
-[beeps] my whole diet up.

269
00:11:31,057 --> 00:11:34,093
-This is it. You ready?
-Look, he smells it like a Beaujolais.

270
00:11:34,160 --> 00:11:35,661
-We're having a good time?
-Yes.

271
00:11:35,728 --> 00:11:37,163
-I need you to tell me.
-Yeah.

272
00:11:37,229 --> 00:11:39,965
[stutters] What are we shooting, a movie?

273
00:11:40,033 --> 00:11:42,866
Are you tired, Howard?
What else you want to know about me?

274
00:11:42,933 --> 00:11:44,533
-Okay, let's get back to you.
-Yeah.

275
00:11:44,570 --> 00:11:46,706
-What were we talking about?
-Cunnilingus.

276
00:11:46,772 --> 00:11:47,707
[camera shutter clicks]

277
00:11:51,066 --> 00:11:55,466
Hi, everybody. Welcome to
What Does it Cost with Jerry Seinfeld.

278
00:11:56,682 --> 00:11:58,584
Jerry, what I'm holding
in my hand right now...

279
00:11:58,651 --> 00:12:00,086
$1.29. $1.79.

280
00:12:00,152 --> 00:12:02,188
-It's $1.89.
-Come on!

281
00:12:02,254 --> 00:12:03,789
-How much is that?
-$2.69.

282
00:12:03,856 --> 00:12:05,758
-Ninety-nine cents.
-$1.49.

283
00:12:05,825 --> 00:12:08,894
$1.39. It's Campbell's
Chicken Noodle Soup, Jerry.

284
00:12:08,961 --> 00:12:10,930
Eighty-nine cents. $1.29.

285
00:12:10,996 --> 00:12:13,833
$2.09 for a [beeps] can of soup.

286
00:12:13,900 --> 00:12:16,433
-Oh, my God. Look at this mother [beeps]
-[Jerry] There he is.

287
00:12:16,469 --> 00:12:18,104
-[Howard] How much is this?
-$2.79.

288
00:12:18,170 --> 00:12:20,306
-$5.79.
-Most men

289
00:12:20,372 --> 00:12:23,242
that have just regular jobs
don't know what these things cost.

290
00:12:23,333 --> 00:12:24,966
Don't make it seem
like I'm out of touch.

291
00:12:25,033 --> 00:12:26,333
-You should be in touch.
-Why?

292
00:12:26,779 --> 00:12:28,481
-[Howard] This is dumb.
-[Jerry] Let's go.

293
00:12:29,866 --> 00:12:33,333
In your city office, do you have
a lot of mementos of your career?

294
00:12:33,385 --> 00:12:35,588
-A couple.
-What do you have hanging on the walls?

295
00:12:35,654 --> 00:12:38,958
I'll tell you the greatest thing
that I've ever achieved in my career.

296
00:12:39,033 --> 00:12:41,033
I was on the cover of Mad Magazine.

297
00:12:41,127 --> 00:12:42,995
-I'm saying, "Hello Neuman."
-Know what's in my office?

298
00:12:43,066 --> 00:12:44,533
-What?
-Mad Magazine,

299
00:12:44,599 --> 00:12:47,599
the cover, I got it blown up
the size of this window.

300
00:12:47,666 --> 00:12:50,002
It's all that mattered to me.
It's the most important thing.

301
00:12:50,069 --> 00:12:52,238
How crazy is it
that we both feel that way?

302
00:12:52,333 --> 00:12:54,400
-That's what I'm saying.
-To me that was it.

303
00:12:54,466 --> 00:12:55,666
You know what it was?

304
00:12:55,733 --> 00:12:58,900
That was when you found out
there were adults that were silly.

305
00:12:58,944 --> 00:13:00,646
I adore Mad Magazine.

306
00:13:03,566 --> 00:13:05,366
"The Judge." Hey, I'm a judge.

307
00:13:05,417 --> 00:13:06,819
-You figured that out?
-Yeah.

308
00:13:07,133 --> 00:13:08,900
To me, America

309
00:13:08,954 --> 00:13:10,489
used to be a place

310
00:13:10,556 --> 00:13:12,057
that made steel

311
00:13:12,224 --> 00:13:13,826
and cars

312
00:13:13,900 --> 00:13:15,833
and had giant department stores.

313
00:13:15,900 --> 00:13:18,300
Now, basically we produce

314
00:13:18,366 --> 00:13:21,733
amateur talent and people
that judge amateur talent.

315
00:13:21,800 --> 00:13:23,300
And I hopped right in on that.

316
00:13:27,273 --> 00:13:28,174
Look at that. That's...

317
00:13:28,240 --> 00:13:29,441
-[Jerry] Nice.
-[Howard] You've got talent.

318
00:13:29,533 --> 00:13:30,966
America's Got Talent, honey.

319
00:13:32,812 --> 00:13:33,712
[laughing]

320
00:13:33,779 --> 00:13:34,914
[tires screeching]

321
00:13:42,833 --> 00:13:44,733
I'm thinking to cut my hair like Jerry.

322
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:46,266
-Really?
-Because it's too much.

323
00:13:46,333 --> 00:13:48,199
-It's too, too much--
-You did this.

324
00:13:48,260 --> 00:13:50,062
I did it already.
I'm gonna do something new.

325
00:13:50,129 --> 00:13:51,764
-Hi, Toni. Are you the hair person?
-I am.

326
00:13:51,831 --> 00:13:54,066
-Yeah, sorry.
-[Jerry] Want to touch him up?

327
00:13:54,133 --> 00:13:56,468
-I think I look good.
-[Jerry] Gotta touch him up.

328
00:13:56,735 --> 00:13:59,138
I would fluff it a little. Fluff it.

329
00:13:59,305 --> 00:14:00,573
-It's getting...
-Pull it out?

330
00:14:00,639 --> 00:14:01,640
Yeah, pull it out.

331
00:14:02,433 --> 00:14:03,599
Come on, fix me up.

332
00:14:04,166 --> 00:14:05,500
[Jerry] I don't know what the look is.

333
00:14:05,544 --> 00:14:07,646
-Is it Slash or Rhea Perlman?
-[Howard sighs]

